This collapse pattern explains three key observations. First, it explains why Detach falls \emph{below} Vanilla: the encoder effectively stops learning, producing representations that degrade under continued LoRA updates to downstream layers. Second, it explains why label content is irrelevant: any auxiliary gradient flow (structured or random) maintains encoder gradient norms at Vanilla-comparable levels, preventing collapse regardless of signal content. Third, it explains the Detach TSR instability ($\sigma = 0.163$): seeds where the collapse is more severe suffer broader performance degradation.

Action and auxiliary gradients are approximately orthogonal throughout training (mean cosine similarity $\approx 0$, conflict rate $\approx 50\%$), ruling out directional guidance---the auxiliary task provides an independent gradient direction rather than steering the encoder toward safer features. This orthogonality is consistent with the collapse prevention interpretation: the auxiliary gradient maintains encoder updates along a complementary subspace, preventing the effective rank of encoder gradients from collapsing.

\subsection{Per-Task Analysis}
\label{sec:per_task}

Seven of ten tasks achieve CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under Vanilla, leaving minimal room for improvement; measured effects concentrate on three physically constrained tasks (T3, T5, T6) where Vanilla CAR falls below 0.58. The overall CAR of 0.780 is dominated by these three hard tasks, which average 0.342 compared to 0.968 for the easy tasks (full breakdown in Appendix~\ref{app:per_task}).

\textbf{Detach is systematically worst on hard tasks.} Detach achieves the lowest CAR on T3 (0.11 vs.\ 0.31--0.38 for other conditions) and T5 (0.05 vs.\ 0.07--0.23), consistent with gradient detachment exacerbating degradation where obstacle proximity is tight. Among gradient-flow conditions, no consistent ordering emerges: on T3, Vanilla (0.38) matches Full OCC (0.37); on T6, Full OCC (0.61) leads while Random OCC (0.55) trails. This reinforces the damage-prevention interpretation---gradient flow prevents Detach-specific degradation but does not systematically improve clearance.

\subsection{Temporal Dynamics and Reference Frame}
\label{sec:temporal}

\textbf{Cutoff$^\dagger$ reveals distinct temporal requirements for TSR and CAR.} Cutoff (gradient flow for 35K of 50K steps) achieves the highest TSR among all conditions (0.915) but loses collision avoidance on the hardest task (T5 CAR 0.00---the worst of any condition on any task). The CAR-preserving effect of gradient flow partially persists after removing auxiliary gradients (Cutoff CAR 0.783 vs.\ Detach 0.746, $+$3.7\,pp), but does not match continuous gradient flow (Full OCC 0.797, $+$1.4\,pp above Cutoff). Early-phase auxiliary gradients thus establish representations that partially resist degradation, but physically constrained tasks require continuous gradient flow throughout training. The three data points---Detach (0\% of steps with gradient flow, CAR 0.746), Cutoff (70\%, CAR 0.783), Full OCC (100\%, CAR 0.797)---suggest a monotonic dose-response relationship between gradient flow duration and CAR, though the limited temporal resolution (three points) precludes fitting a specific functional form.

\textbf{Egocentric depth matches Vanilla despite richer spatial content.} Depth (4 seeds) achieves CAR of 0.780 $\pm$ 0.039---identical to Vanilla (0.780 $\pm$ 0.012)---despite providing per-pixel geometric information directly aligned with collision avoidance. Three distinct label types (structured BEV occupancy, random binary maps, egocentric depth) yield comparable CAR when gradient flow is maintained, reinforcing that gradient flow presence is the operative factor. Depth exhibits the highest cross-seed CAR variance among gradient-flow conditions ($\sigma = 0.039$), with hard-task ranges of 0.20--0.78 (T3) and 0.16--0.84 (T6), consistent with auxiliary supervision increasing seed sensitivity regardless of label content.

\label{sec:why_gradient}

The gradient analysis in Section~\ref{sec:gradient_dynamics} identifies the operative mechanism: auxiliary gradient flow is sufficient to prevent encoder gradient collapse during LoRA fine-tuning. Without auxiliary gradients, Detach OCC encoder gradient norms fall to 1--13\% of Vanilla, effectively freezing early encoder layers while downstream LoRA adapters continue to update. This creates a representational mismatch: the encoder produces increasingly stale features that the LoRA layers must compensate for, degrading both collision avoidance ($-$3.4\,pp) and task execution stability ($\sigma = 0.163$).

Three properties of this collapse prevention mechanism explain the experimental patterns:
(1)~\emph{Content independence}: structured occupancy and random binary labels maintain encoder gradient norms at 0.87--1.14$\times$ of Vanilla levels; egocentric depth achieves equivalent behavioral outcomes (CAR $\approx$ Vanilla), consistent with the same mechanism. This explains why label content is secondary (CAR spread $\leq$ 1.7\,pp among gradient-flow conditions).
(2)~\emph{Orthogonal regularization}: action and auxiliary gradients are approximately orthogonal (cosine similarity $\approx 0$), so the auxiliary gradient provides an independent update direction that maintains the effective rank of encoder updates without interfering with action learning. This mechanism is analogous to deep supervision~\cite{lee2015deeply}, where intermediate classifiers in deep networks (e.g., GoogLeNet auxiliary heads) prevent gradient vanishing in early layers by injecting gradient signal at intermediate points. Our auxiliary head serves the same structural role: it injects gradient flow into the vision encoder, preventing LoRA fine-tuning from silencing early-layer updates.
(3)~\emph{Temporal sensitivity}: Cutoff@35K maintains Vanilla-level performance (CAR 0.783 vs.\ 0.780), suggesting that 35K steps of auxiliary gradient flow suffices to establish encoder robustness that persists after withdrawal. A comprehensive dose-response across cutoff durations (15K/25K/35K/45K) is under investigation.

\subsection{The Detach Degradation: BatchNorm Artifact or Gradient Flow Effect?}
\label{sec:why_detach}

Detach OCC falls 3.4\,pp below Vanilla despite identical architecture and forward computation to Full OCC. The auxiliary head's BatchNorm2d layers update running statistics at every forward pass regardless of gradient routing; with \texttt{stop\_gradient}, the encoder bears this statistical perturbation without compensating auxiliary gradients. This forward-pass interference makes Detach worse than Vanilla: the encoder absorbs the cost of the auxiliary pathway without receiving any training benefit. The TSR instability under Detach ($\sigma = 0.163$ vs.\ $< 0.050$ for all other conditions) confirms that gradient detachment destabilizes optimization broadly.

A discriminating test replaces the auxiliary head's BatchNorm2d layers with LayerNorm---which normalizes per-sample and does not maintain cross-batch running statistics---while keeping gradient detachment (\texttt{stop\_gradient}) unchanged. If Detach+LN recovers to Vanilla-level CAR ($\approx 0.780$), the 3.4\,pp deficit is a BatchNorm artifact; if it remains near Detach ($\approx 0.746$), gradient flow absence is the genuine cause. A mixed outcome (CAR between 0.746 and 0.780) would indicate both factors contribute.

