\subsection{Factorial Decomposition}
\label{sec:mechanism}

The four core conditions form a $2{\times}2$ factorial crossing gradient flow (ON/OFF) with label content (structured/random). Table~\ref{tab:factorial} arranges the CAR values.

\textbf{Gradient flow dominates.} The column effect---gradient ON minus OFF---is $+$5.1\,pp with structured labels (Full OCC 0.797 vs.\ Detach 0.746) and $+$0.9\,pp with random/no labels (Random OCC 0.789 vs.\ Vanilla 0.780). The dominant pattern is not that gradient flow \emph{improves} CAR above Vanilla, but that its \emph{absence} degrades CAR below Vanilla: Detach falls 3.4\,pp below Vanilla, while gradient-flow conditions remain within 1.7\,pp of it.

\textbf{Label content contributes marginally.} The row effect---structured minus random/none---is $+$0.8\,pp when gradient flows (Full OCC vs.\ Random OCC) and $-$3.4\,pp when it does not (Detach vs.\ Vanilla). The latter conflates label content with forward-pass interference: Detach runs the auxiliary head, introducing BatchNorm statistics that the encoder cannot adapt to without auxiliary gradients, causing it to fall \emph{below} Vanilla rather than matching it (Section~\ref{sec:why_detach}).

